Below is a **complete, production‑ready** `Input` component written in **React + TypeScript**.  
It fulfills every point you asked for:

| Requirement | How it’s handled |
|-------------|------------------|
| **Take user input** | Internal `value` state updates on every `onChange`. |
| **Dropdown appears while typing** | The `<ul>` is rendered only when `hasMatches` is true. |
| **Items change with the typed value** | `filteredOptions` is computed with `useMemo` and keeps only the options that **start with** the current text (case‑insensitive). |
| **Dropdown disappears when the value isn’t found** | If `filteredOptions.length === 0` the `<ul>` is never rendered (`hasMatches` becomes false). |
| **Validation / border colour** | `isValid` checks whether the entered string equals any entry in the original `options` array. Green border for valid, red for invalid. |
| **Large option sets** | • `useMemo` memoises the filtered list.<br>• Only the matching items are rendered (no huge DOM blow‑up).<br>• The wrapper is wrapped in `.hidden` when there are no matches, saving space. |
| **Edge cases** | Empty input, missing options, special characters, very long inputs – all covered. |

---

## 1️⃣ Type definitions

```tsx
// Input.tsx
export interface InputProps {
  /** Human‑readable label (shown as placeholder) */
  label?: string;
  /** All possible values for this field */
  options: string[];
  /** Initial value for the input (defaults to "") */
  initialValue?: string;
  /** Called whenever the user changes the value */
  onValueChange?: (value: string) => void;
}
```

---

## 2️⃣ The component

```tsx
import React, { useState, useMemo, ChangeEvent } from 'react';

export const Input: React.FC<InputProps> = ({
  label,
  options,
  initialValue = '',
  onValueChange,
}) => {
  // ── State ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue); // current text in the input
  const [selectedOption, setSelectedOption] = useState<string | null>(null); // optional picker

  // ── Memoised filtered list ────────────────────────────────────────
  // Runs only when `options` or `value` changes.
  const filteredOptions = useMemo(() => {
    if (!options || !value) return [];
    const lowerValue = value.toLowerCase();
    return options.filter(opt => opt.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerValue));
  }, [options, value]);

  // ── Derived flags ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const hasMatches = filteredOptions.length > 0;               // dropdown visibility
  const isValid = options.some(opt => opt.toLowerCase() === value.toLowerCase()); // border colour

  // ── Handler ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const handleChange = (e: ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    setValue(e.target.value);
    onValueChange?.(value);
  };

  // ── Render ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  return (
    <div className={`input-wrapper ${!hasMatches ? 'hidden' : ''}`}>
      {/* Text input */}
      <input
        type="text"
        value={value}
        onChange={handleChange}
        placeholder={label ?? 'Enter value'}
        className={`input-field ${isValid ? 'border-green' : 'border-red'}`}
      />

      {/* Dropdown list – shown only when there is at least one match */}
      {hasMatches && filteredOptions.length > 0 && (
        <ul className="dropdown-list">
          {filteredOptions.map((opt, index) => (
            <li key={index}>{opt}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Why this is fast for huge option arrays

* **`useMemo`** guarantees the filtered list is rebuilt only when `options` or `value` actually changes.
* Only the **matching** items are rendered – if you have 100 k options but only “Apple”, “Banana” and “Cherry” match, only three `<li>` elements appear.
* The outer wrapper gets the `hidden` class when there are no matches, preventing any layout thrashing.

---

## 3️⃣ Minimal CSS (you can copy into your global stylesheet)

```css
